    {
      "id": "8mnd165",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Chemical reactions represent processes where chemical bonds are broken and formed to yield new substances.",
        "Chemical equations must be balanced to obey the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
        "Reactions are categorized into key types based on energy changes, oxygen transfer, and combination patterns.",
        "Oxidation and reduction occur simultaneously as redox processes during chemical transformations.",
        "Corrosion and rancidity are everyday impacts of oxidation that alter metals and fats/oils."
      ],
      "id": "u1fz7ba"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d2siqrm",
      "content": "• A chemical reaction is a process in which one or more substances are transformed into new substances."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pn5vdrn",
      "content": "• The substances that take part in a reaction are called reactants."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nz9tx5g",
      "content": "• The substances formed in a reaction are called products."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cowujc1",
      "content": "• Chemical reactions involve breaking and forming of chemical bonds."
    },
    {
      "id": "cdc9apj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o8m3g89",
      "content": "A chemical equation is a symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using chemical formulas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0mzllpa",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "shzhe0i",
      "content": "2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "zpc11sm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Balanced Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "omqea5r",
      "content": "A balanced chemical equation has an equal number of atoms of each element on both sides."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x820uw8",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q7yztpq",
      "content": "CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "mjroktf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Indicators of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "n51cf8l",
      "content": "• Change in colour"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2bgy0zt",
      "content": "• Change in temperature"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gh6u17x",
      "content": "• Formation of gas"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "who1fs7",
      "content": "• Formation of precipitate"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xh4svhm",
      "content": "• Emission of light or sound"
    },
    {
      "id": "imylo8y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Types of Chemical Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "6w1ouiy",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>General Form</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Combination Reaction</p>",
            "<p>A + B → AB</p>",
            "<p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Decomposition Reaction</p>",
            "<p>AB → A + B</p>",
            "<p>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Displacement Reaction</p>",
            "<p>A + BC → AC + B</p>",
            "<p>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Double Displacement Reaction</p>",
            "<p>AB + CD → AD + CB</p>",
            "<p>AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "8b38asr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Oxidation and Reduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4z0aoyg",
      "content": "Oxidation: Addition of oxygen or loss of hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Exothermic Reactions",
        "Endothermic Reactions"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Heat Energy",
          "Released into the surroundings",
          "Absorbed from the surroundings"
        ],
        [
          "Temperature Change",
          "Surrounding temperature increases",
          "Surrounding temperature decreases"
        ],
        [
          "Key Example Processes",
          "Combustion, Respiration",
          "Photosynthesis, Thermal Decomposition"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "oqzwd1v"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n6d9yue",
      "content": "Reduction: Removal of oxygen or gain of hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g4wd3w4",
      "content": "These reactions together are called redox reactions."
    },
    {
      "id": "zysko3f",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "guwnq95",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Exothermic Reaction</p>",
            "<p>Releases heat energy</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Endothermic Reaction</p>",
            "<p>Absorbs heat energy</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ylo6xkt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Corrosion"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nxniqjp",
      "content": "Corrosion is the gradual destruction of metals due to chemical reactions with the environment. Rusting of iron is the most common example."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wq6z51v",
      "content": "Example of rusting:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pydy7s5",
      "content": "Iron + Oxygen + Water → Rust"
    },
    {
      "id": "xs35wy8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. Rancidity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ezppqq8",
      "content": "Rancidity refers to the oxidation of fats and oils in food which causes unpleasant smell and taste. It can be prevented by storing food in airtight containers."
    },
    {
      "id": "xf1k0jz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10. Catalysts"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Questions on balancing chemical equations frequently test the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
        "Differentiating between oxidation (gain of oxygen/loss of hydrogen) and reduction (loss of oxygen/gain of hydrogen) is a recurring topic.",
        "Identifying indicators of chemical reactions and distinguishing exothermic from endothermic processes are crucial for competitive exams.",
        "Role of catalysts and methods to prevent corrosion and rancidity are commonly asked in descriptive and objective questions."
      ],
      "id": "57j6juu"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4j9pzjk",
      "content": "A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without being consumed in the reaction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nrfda3w",
      "content": "Examples: Platinum, Iron catalyst in Haber process"
    },
    {
      "id": "mgl2r0d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "at6pdq0",
      "content": "• Chemical equations represent chemical reactions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r22o8w4",
      "content": "• Rusting of iron is a common example of corrosion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d86bej5",
      "content": "• Redox reactions involve oxidation and reduction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2r5ogak",
      "content": "• Exothermic reactions release heat energy."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}